  "summary": "Comic drawing with uniform line weight, flat colors, no hatching and documentary background detail — Tintin's clarity.",
  "description": "Hergé's Tintin established the ligne claire: strong uniform ink lines, flat planes of color, absence of shadows and chiaroscuro, and rigorously researched, realistic backgrounds that make the cartoon figures coexist with the real world. Joost Swarte named the style in 1977; masters from Jacobs to Chomet and designers across Europe extended it. Its legibility made it a staple of European graphic design.",
